<cell>
<cname>home</cname>
<text>This web illustrates the use of Mathweb's builtin functions rref() and gaussj() for manipulating matrices.

The cell 'row_reduce' contains a script which demonstrates the use of the Mathweb builtin function rref(), which converts a matrix to its reduced row echelon form.

The cell 'inverta' has a script which illustrates the use of the Mathweb builtin function gaussj() which, among other things, inverts a square matrix.

The cell 'solveab' demonstrates the use of gaussj() to solve the matrix equation:
        Mx=b
where M is a 4X4 matrix, and b is 4X1 column matrix.</text>
<val>0.00</val></cell>
<cell>
<cname>inputa</cname>
<text>12 8 -7 14 
-3 21 -9 4 
-1 -3 -6 17 
5.5 6 -3 -1 
</text>
<script>set inname to 'inputa'
for(rdx=0 to (%1-1)) {
    for(cdx=0 to (%2-1)) {
         in(a01[rdx*%2+cdx])
    } #endfor
} #endfor
outputa(%1,%2)
</script></cell>
<cell>
<cname>inputb</cname>
<text>7
-3
1
4
</text>
<script>set inname to 'inputb'
for(rdx=0 to (%1-1)) {
    for(cdx=0 to (%2-1)) {
         in(b1[rdx*%2+cdx])
    } #endfor
} #endfor
outputb(%1,%2)
</script></cell>
<cell>
<cname>inverta</cname>
<text>To generate the inverse of a square matrix, gaussj() may be called with two arguments:

      A quoted cell name, which tells which cell is the first cell    
      in the matrix to be inverted.
    
      An expression which evaluates to the number of rows
      and columns in the square matrix.

The cells of the matrix are laid out in row-column order in the sequence of cells beginning with the quoted cellname.
</text>
<script>out("\nInput matrix")
inputa(4,4)
if(gaussj("a01",4)=0) {
    out("\nInverse matrix")
    outputa(4,4)
} else {
    out("\nMatrix is singular")
}
</script></cell>
